<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  An entitlement is added in the same commit as the feature that needs it and no earlier.
	  See docs/06-privacy-and-entitlements.md.

	  Deliberately ABSENT: network server, Photos, Camera, Microphone, Location, Bluetooth,
	  USB, Downloads folder, Apple Events, JIT, and every temporary exception.

	  **The no-network guarantee changed shape when sync arrived.** For its first year this
	  file held no network-client entitlement, so the sandbox refused outbound connections at
	  the kernel boundary — "this app makes no network requests" was verifiable, not a
	  promise. iCloud sync is a network feature; the kernel-enforced version of the guarantee
	  ends with it. What remains true, and what Settings says in the same words: the only
	  endpoint is Apple's CloudKit, the only payload is the user's own library in the user's
	  own private database, sync is off until turned on, and no analytics, telemetry, or
	  third-party service exists in the binary.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- CloudKit, and nothing else on the network. Added in the same commit as sync. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The user's own private database, in this app's own container. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.com.elephruit.Elephruit</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Silent pushes: how a change on the iPhone reaches an open Mac without polling. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!-- Atomic handoff files shared only with the bundled Safari Web Extension. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(ELEPHRUIT_APP_GROUP)</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Open and save panels: import, export, and attaching files. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Security-scoped bookmarks for files the user keeps outside the app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  Reading and writing the calendar.

	  This entitlement lets the sandbox reach CalendarAgent at all. The TCC prompt is separate and is
	  triggered only when the user turns the feature on in Settings — until then the app holds an
	  inert provider and never touches EventKit.

	  EventKit has no read-only tier: its only requests are full access and write-only, so any app
	  that reads must ask for full access. Phase D used only the read half and proved it with a test
	  asserting that no mutating call existed anywhere. The calendar module uses both halves, so that
	  test was replaced rather than deleted, by three narrower properties `CalendarWriteSafetyTests`
	  still enforces: writes take an `EventDraft` and nothing else, so the CRM has nowhere to leak
	  through; mutating EventKit calls exist in exactly three named methods; and nothing is saved or
	  deleted across a series without an explicit scope.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  The People module: reading the address book.

	  This entitlement lets the sandbox reach the Contacts daemon at all. The TCC prompt is separate
	  and is triggered only when the user turns the integration on in Settings — until then the app
	  holds an inert `NoContactsProvider` and never touches `CNContactStore`.

	  Contacts, unlike EventKit, has a single access tier: granting read access grants write access
	  with it, and the user has no way to give less. Elephruit never writes. `ContactsProviding` has
	  no write method, and `ContactsWriteSafetyTests` fails if the adapter ever reaches past it to
	  `CNSaveRequest` — and a second test fails if the adapter fetches a key nothing displays.

	  Still deliberately ABSENT: network client, network server, iCloud, CloudKit, Photos,
	  Camera, Microphone, Location, Bluetooth, USB, Apple Events, JIT, and every temporary exception.
	  Business-card scanning needs no camera entitlement: Continuity Camera is presented by the
	  system, which hands back a file, and Vision reads it on-device.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  The Tasks module: linking to Apple Reminders.

	  This entitlement lets the sandbox reach the reminders store at all. The TCC prompt is separate
	  and is triggered only when the user turns the integration on in Settings — until then the app
	  holds an inert `NoRemindersProvider` and never touches `EKEventStore`.

	  **This is the first integration that writes**, and the difference is deliberate rather than an
	  erosion. The calendar and the address book are read-only by construction: their protocols have
	  no write method, so writing is a compile error. That is not available here, because a task
	  manager linked to Reminders that cannot tick a reminder off is not linked to anything.

	  So the guarantee changes shape rather than disappearing. Every write is a `ReminderWrite`
	  value that can be previewed, logged, and asserted about; `RemindersProviding.apply(_:)` is the
	  single door it goes through; and `RemindersWriteSafetyTests` fails if any other method in the
	  EventKit adapter names a mutating call, or if a deletion can be reached without an explicit
	  `LinkedDeletionChoice`.

	  EventKit offers no read-only or write-only tier for reminders — `requestWriteOnlyAccessToEvents()`
	  is events-only — so full access is the only request there is, and the user has no way to grant
	  less. The usage string in Info.plist says what is actually done with it.

	  iCloud synchronisation of the user's reminders is the operating system's business. This app
	  never asks for an Apple ID, holds no iCloud entitlement, and speaks to no private API: it
	  writes to the store macOS gives it and the OS carries the change wherever the user has
	  configured it to go.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.reminders</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
